On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:47:32 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I like this a lot.

Thanks. It is posted as "[PATCH v5 0/5] lab-process-mail: Add
lab-process-maild" [1]. Patch 4 adds the exit status. Patch 5 runs
"lab-process-maild drain" under daemon(8) -R, and drain runs
lab-process-mail again at once while it exits 75. Under the real
daemon(8) with a 60 second interval, the tests answer three messages
back to back.

> That sounds good to me. It's a clear contract.

lab-process-mail documents it in its header comment, and
lab-process-maild(8) documents drain.

I was wrong that "just process-email" would need to accept 75. Its
recipe, from "[PATCH v3 0/6] Add just process-email" [2], runs its own
loop and calls claude directly, never lab-process-mail. Nothing but
drain runs lab-process-mail, so no other caller changes.

> The one thing I don't understand is "A run by hand that clears the inbox
> still exits 0." By that I suppose you mean if I run lab-process-mail on
> an inbox with size 0 or 1, then it exits 0? That's fine. But I assume
> there's no special "being run in daemon" logic. If there's a message at
> the end of the run, it exits 75.

Yes, that is it exactly. There is no daemon mode. It exits 0 if the
inbox is empty at the end of the run, so an inbox of 0 or 1 gives 0
once that one message is answered. It exits 75 if a message is left
after it answered one, and 1 on failure, however it was started. I
meant only that a run by hand which empties the inbox looks the same
as before.

> Then it just gets into an infinite loop, right? Until we address the bug
> report about stuck messages?

Yes, but a slow and cheap one. A stuck message makes its run exit 1,
which ends drain. daemon(8) starts drain again after the interval, and
lab-process-mail fetches, sees the held message is still the oldest,
and exits 1 before the agent starts. That repeats once per interval,
with no agent pass, no notice, and one error line in the log, until
the message is answered or archived or
~/var/db/lab-process-mail/stuck is removed.

The real cost is that the held message blocks the rest of the inbox.
"[BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck" [3] lists the fixes. Fix 3 tags
the stuck message and skips it, so later mail keeps moving and the
loop ends by itself. I recommend that as the next patch, after v5,
since it changes which message is answered and deserves its own
review. Say the word and I will send it.

> Makes sense to me. Show me some examples of this convention being used
> in FreeBSD.

These are all on 15.1, from the man pages and /usr/sbin/periodic.

Base callers branch on 75 itself. lockf(1) exits EX_TEMPFAIL when the
lock is held by another process, and periodic(8) acts on that status
to report a run still in progress rather than a failure:

	lockf -s -t 0 "${lockfile}" /bin/sh $0 LOCKED "$arg"
	case $? in
	0) ;;
	73) #EX_CANTCREATE
	    ...
	75) #EX_TEMPFAIL
	    echo "$host ${arg##*/} prior run still in progress" | \
	        output_pipe $arg "$PERIODIC"

sendmail(8) lists EX_TEMPFAIL as "Message could not be sent
immediately, but was queued", and sysexits(3) defines it as "not
really an error", where "the request should be reattempted later".
mail.local(8) returns that kind of temporary error for a mailbox over
quota, unless -b asks for a permanent one, so sendmail keeps the
message queued and retries it.

The wider convention, a non-zero status that is not a failure and
tells the caller what to do next, is everywhere in base:

- make(1) -q: "exit 0 if the specified targets are up to date, and 1
  otherwise", so a caller learns whether there is work left without
  doing it.
- grep(1): 0 for a match, 1 for none, >1 for an error. diff(1) and
  cmp(1) use the same split.
- periodic(8) scripts: 0 for nothing notable, 1 for notable output, 2
  for bad configuration, and >2 for output that must not be masked.
  periodic reads the status to decide what to show.

One honest difference is that 75 conventionally means "try again
later", and drain tries again at once. The sysexits(3) wording still
fits, since the run did its job and a retry is expected, and the
operator still controls the delay after the inbox empties.

